# Input graph for deepin-community__lv2__scripts_lv2_check_specification.py___has_property — written during translation.
# Covers: several solutions for one (subject, predicate) pair (ex:s1/ex:p1
# has two values), a single-solution pair (ex:s1/ex:p2), the zero-solution
# case both ways (a predicate never used on the subject, ex:s1/ex:p3; a
# subject absent from the graph entirely, ex:s3/ex:p1), and neighbouring
# triples that must NOT match (ex:p2 on ex:s1 is not ex:p1; ex:p1 on ex:s2
# is not on ex:s1 -- proves the read is scoped to the exact pair).
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .

ex:s1 ex:p1 "v1" .
ex:s1 ex:p1 "v2" .
ex:s1 ex:p2 "other" .
ex:s2 ex:p1 "v3" .

# Repair of 2026-10-31 -- falsy values.  The repository asks
# `_has_property(model, subject, rdfs.label)` of every typed subject of an
# LV2 spec (lv2_check_specification.py:164, :183), precisely to catch a
# spec that gets its labels wrong: an empty label is a value, and
# `value(...) is not None` says True for it.  A rewrite through the truth of
# the value (`bool(m{ ... }.first())`, `not value(...)`) says False for "",
# 0 and false -- these three subjects are what tells them apart.
ex:s4 ex:p1 "" .
ex:s5 ex:p1 0 .
ex:s6 ex:p1 false .
